# tests/modifiers/xmode.rxt — module `modifiers` (MOD-0.5c/d): single `x`
# (extended mode) OUTSIDE character classes, per [MOD-0.5a] ruling 4's
# measured skip set and tests/probes/probe_mod05.c/probe_mod05b.c's
# controlled census. This file is deliberately narrow: it exercises the
# LEXER-VISIBLE behaviour (whitespace skipped, `#`-comments to end of line,
# escaped whitespace stays literal, class interiors untouched by single
# `x`) with representative subjects, NOT the full byte census — that
# census lives in the probes (tests/probes/probe_mod05b.c's controlled
# result: skip set {09,0A,0B,0C,0D,20,85}, NOT \s's set — 0x85 NEL is
# skipped and \s does not contain it) and is design evidence, not a
# per-byte corpus.
#
# ORACLE: every block here is python-VERIFIABLE. Python's `re.X` (verbose)
# mode happens to agree with libpcre2/pcrec on these specific cells:
# whitespace-skip outside classes, `#`-comment-to-newline, escaped-space-
# stays-literal, and "single x never touches class interiors" are all
# shared behaviour (python's own verbose-mode docs make the same class-
# interior exception) — confirmed directly against python3 `re` for the
# exact subjects used below. (Python's whitespace set differs at the
# margins — no NEL — but none of these subjects probe that margin; the
# byte-exact skip census is the probes' job, not this file's.)
#
# FORMAT LIMITATION: the `.rxt` format is strictly line-oriented with ZERO
# escaping in the `pattern` line (docs/testing.md: "no quoting, no escaping
# — write the pattern exactly as PCRE would see it"), so a pattern cannot
# contain a literal embedded newline BYTE. That means the "comment runs to
# the next newline, then a real atom resumes" cell (tests/probes/
# probe_mod05.c's `(?x)a#c\nb` — an actual LF inside the C string literal)
# cannot be written here at all; the probe is the only place that measures
# it. What CAN be written and is below is the weaker-but-real cell "a
# comment consumes everything to the end of the PATTERN, with no trailing
# atom" — it still confirms `#` opens a comment, just not the newline-stop
# specifically.
#
# THE D33 §9.3 RECORD: run against the MOD-0.5a-gate binary via
#   PCREC=<binary> bash tests/harness/run.sh tests/modifiers/xmode.rxt
# every block's pcrec compile fails with "requires module 'modifiers'" —
# 7 cases, 0 passed, 4 distinct pattern-compile failures (measured
# 2026-08-12; `(?x)` is not wired to a lexer yet — that is slice 3,
# [MOD-0.5d]).

# ---- whitespace skipped outside classes -----------------------------------

pattern (?x)a b
features modifiers
m "ab" 0 2
n "a b"

# ---- escaped whitespace stays literal --------------------------------------

pattern (?x)a\ b
features modifiers
m "a b" 0 3
n "ab"

# ---- #-comment consumes to end of pattern (the newline-stop cell is not
# expressible in this line-oriented format — see the FORMAT LIMITATION note
# above; it is measured instead in tests/probes/probe_mod05.c) -------------

pattern (?x)ab#trailing comment
features modifiers
m "ab" 0 2

# ---- single x NEVER touches class interiors: the space stays a member -----

pattern (?x)[a b]
features modifiers
m " " 0 1
m "a" 0 1
